  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is doctor a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Medicine is a real, licensed profession with structural, long-term demand that will not disappear. It is also, honestly, one of the longest and most financially demanding professional runways available in India.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both things are true at once, and most articles on this keyword pick only one side — either the "noble, always-respected, always-secure" pitch, or a cynical "doctors are exploited and underpaid" warning. Neither is the full picture.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Choosing MBBS purely because it is the highest-prestige option your NEET score allows, without testing your actual tolerance for clinical work, resident-hour pressure, and a decade-long runway to settled income, is a weaker bet today than the same decision looked twenty years ago — the fee gap has widened, and NEET-PG competition has intensified. But a student with genuine clinical interest, a realistic fee plan, and a deliberate specialisation strategy still has one of the most stable, respected, and eventually well-paying career paths in the country. The decision is not "doctor yes or no." It is "which specific route into medicine, funded how, and specialising toward what."
    </p>
  </div>

