  <h2 id="training-path">The MBBS training path, start to registration</h2>

  <p>
    MBBS is a 5.5-year programme — 4.5 years of academic and clinical coursework followed by a mandatory 1-year rotating internship — regulated by the National Medical Commission (NMC), which replaced the Medical Council of India.
  </p>

  <p>
    Entry is through NEET-UG, the same exam that also gates BDS, BAMS, BHMS, and BVSc admissions at lower cutoffs. After the internship, a graduate registers with the NMC and the relevant state medical council to practise independently as a general physician — though, as the next section covers, most graduates then pursue further specialisation before settling into a long-term career track.
  </p>

  <h2 id="neet-pg">NEET-PG and MD/MS: the real postgraduate competition</h2>

  <p>
    A postgraduate MD or MS is where most of the real specialisation, income growth, and clinical depth in medicine actually sits — and it is genuinely one of the tightest bottlenecks in Indian medical education.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
