  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      Do not treat NEET-PG as a formality once you decide you want a specific branch. With the most sought-after clinical branches at reputed government colleges needing an AIR in the low thousands, and a large share of the remaining seats sitting in deemed or private colleges with steep fees on top of the MBBS cost already paid, this needs real preparation time and a genuine backup plan — a government Medical Officer role, a less competitive branch, or a private-college PG seat weighed carefully against its cost — rather than an assumption that a good branch will simply happen.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    For doctors who pursue a further super-specialisation — DM in fields like cardiology or neurology, or MCh in surgical super-specialities — a separate NEET-SS exam gates roughly 2,000-2,500 seats nationally for a further 3-year programme, an even tighter bottleneck than NEET-PG itself.
  </p>

