  <h2 id="private-practice">Private practice: the real setup math</h2>

  <p>
    Independent practice or nursing-home ownership is the outcome many families picture as the eventual reward for the long training runway — and it is also the outcome that takes the longest, and needs the most capital, to actually reach.
  </p>

  <p>
    Setting up a private clinic or nursing home needs real upfront capital — consultation space, diagnostic equipment appropriate to the specialty (far higher for radiology, cardiology, or surgical specialities than for general practice), clinical establishment registration, biomedical waste management compliance, staff, and typically several years before referrals and repeat patients build a reliable income. Most doctors work as a hospital consultant, a government Medical Officer, or an associate under an established practice first — building case volume, a referral network, and a visible track record before taking on the capital risk of an independent setup.
  </p>

  <h2 id="abroad">MBBS abroad: the FMGE/NExT risk</h2>

  <p>
    For students who cannot clear a competitive NEET-UG rank, or whose families cannot fund a private Indian MBBS seat, studying MBBS abroad is a real, commonly chosen route — and it deserves an honest look, not a blanket dismissal or a blanket endorsement.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What genuinely works about it</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Countries like Georgia, Russia, the Philippines, and Kyrgyzstan typically charge roughly ₹15-40 lakh total for an MBBS-equivalent degree, well below a private Indian MBBS.</li>
        <li>Admission itself does not depend on a competitive NEET-UG rank the way an Indian seat does — a qualifying NEET score is usually enough to be eligible.</li>
        <li>Some of these programmes are internationally recognised medical degrees, genuinely useful for practising in certain other countries, not only India.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">The risk that gets underplayed</div>
      <ul>
        <li>To practise in India, a foreign medical graduate must clear India's licensing exam for foreign graduates — historically the FMGE, now transitioning into the NExT framework under the NMC.</li>
        <li>The historical pass rate on this licensing exam has been low across multiple cycles, meaning a meaningful share of foreign medical graduates cannot clear it, sometimes across several attempts.</li>
        <li>College quality, clinical exposure, and language of instruction vary sharply between institutions and countries, and are worth verifying directly rather than trusting an agent's brochure.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      A lower fee abroad does not by itself buy a licence to practise in India. Before committing, verify the specific college's recognition status with the NMC, the current structure and recent pass-rate data for the licensing exam a graduate from that country will need to clear, and have a real backup plan if that exam takes more than one attempt — treat this the same way this site treats any high-risk study-abroad loan decision, not as a simple cost-saving shortcut.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the ₹15-40 lakh cost is being funded through an education loan, treat that loan as a genuinely high-risk commitment, because repayment depends on clearing a licensing exam with a historically low pass rate, not on a guaranteed job the way some other abroad-study loans at least partly assume. Have a real backup plan before you take the loan: a second or third licensing-exam attempt budgeted for, and a fallback income route (a non-clinical, MBBS-adjacent role, or practising in the country of study if that is genuinely viable) if the licence does not come through on the first try.
    </p>
  </div>

